<http://data.silknow.org/object/b65ee7c1-ae7d-3088-a0d2-a3f9380ff9d5>
        a                              ecrm:E22_Man-Made_Object ;
        rdfs:comment                   "Linen and cotton ground (fustian), embroidered in polychrome silks, Cretan feather, outline, chain, satin and stem stitches with French knots, diaper pattern of leaf shapes forming diamonds which enclose birds and groups of carnations, floral scroll border."@en , "Skirt border, linen and cotton with silk thread, Crete, 1700-1800"@en , "Traditional Cretan embroidery is often very highly coloured and uses a variety of stitching techniques. Sometimes patterns were drawn freehand onto the fabric, but  they were also worked out by counting the threads in the fabric. \n\nEmbroiderers embellished textiles used for church decorations, pillows, valances and hems of dresses using designs drawn from the rich mythology of Ancient Crete and the Minoan, Byzantine, and Italian cultures that have all influenced the history of the island. Typically they feature complex floral designs, mermaids, double-headed eagles, winged snakes, and other animals and birds.\n\nThis embroidered border was collected by Thomas Sandwith, British Consul-General in Crete from 1870 to 1885."@en ;
        ecrm:P138i_has_representation  <http://data.silknow.org/image/3c6f05e4-8a75-3ba4-8ff8-38ca447d4c44> , <http://data.silknow.org/image/0adef67f-eebd-3b1f-8620-002b985b1f8c> ;
        ecrm:P3_has_note               "Traditional Cretan embroidery is often very highly coloured and uses a variety of stitching techniques. Sometimes patterns were drawn freehand onto the fabric, but  they were also worked out by counting the threads in the fabric. \n\nEmbroiderers embellished textiles used for church decorations, pillows, valances and hems of dresses using designs drawn from the rich mythology of Ancient Crete and the Minoan, Byzantine, and Italian cultures that have all influenced the history of the island. Typically they feature complex floral designs, mermaids, double-headed eagles, winged snakes, and other animals and birds.\n\nThis embroidered border was collected by Thomas Sandwith, British Consul-General in Crete from 1870 to 1885."@en , "Skirt border, linen and cotton with silk thread, Crete, 1700-1800"@en , "Linen and cotton ground (fustian), embroidered in polychrome silks, Cretan feather, outline, chain, satin and stem stitches with French knots, diaper pattern of leaf shapes forming diamonds which enclose birds and groups of carnations, floral scroll border."@en ;
        dc:identifier                  "2057&D/1, 2-1876" ;
        <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#sameAs>
                <https://collections.vam.ac.uk/item/O169285> .

